WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.033 --> 00:00:02.400
-Welcome back to "Late Night."

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:03.834
We're here with Michael Shannon.
You are not just an actor.

00:00:03.834 --> 00:00:05.400
You're an accomplished musician.
You were just back in Chicago

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:07.767
doing some shows
with your friend Jason.

00:00:07.767 --> 00:00:08.834
Which one here is Jason?

00:00:08.834 --> 00:00:11.601
-Jason Narducy.
He's an amazing bass player.

00:00:11.601 --> 00:00:12.801
He plays with Bob Mould.

00:00:12.801 --> 00:00:14.701
He also has his own band,
Split Single.

00:00:14.701 --> 00:00:16.367
He's incredible.
-And you guys do --

00:00:16.367 --> 00:00:17.667
When you perform, you do --

00:00:17.667 --> 00:00:19.601
you pick an album
and do every song off an album.

00:00:19.601 --> 00:00:21.300
-Yes, sir.
This time we did

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:24.033
a Neil Young album
called "Zuma."

00:00:24.033 --> 00:00:26.033
-Okay.
-Did a "Cortez the Killer"

00:00:26.033 --> 00:00:28.934
that was over 20 minutes long.
-[ Laughs ]

00:00:28.934 --> 00:00:31.467
Do you feel like that was
an album that the crowd knew,

00:00:31.467 --> 00:00:34.067
or were they just sort of
along for the ride?

00:00:34.067 --> 00:00:36.000
-It was about 50/50.
I mean, I think there were

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:38.133
some people there 'cause of
like, "Oh, look, it's him,"

00:00:38.133 --> 00:00:40.834
and there were some people like,
"I love this album, man."

00:00:40.834 --> 00:00:42.000
You know?
-[ Laughs ] That's good.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:44.000
Nice to have a mix
when you're doing a show.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:46.701
You had a band.
How old was your --

00:00:46.701 --> 00:00:48.667
Your first band was called
Jehovah's what?

00:00:48.667 --> 00:00:50.868
-Jehovah's Suspects.
-Okay, Jehovah's Suspects.

00:00:50.868 --> 00:00:53.133
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah, yeah.

00:00:53.133 --> 00:00:55.968
That was
when I was 15 years old.

00:00:55.968 --> 00:00:58.434
I was living
in Lexington, Kentucky.

00:00:58.434 --> 00:01:01.734
And my best friend at the time
was a fella named James Brown.

00:01:01.734 --> 00:01:02.834
-Okay.
-Not the James Brown.

00:01:02.834 --> 00:01:04.334
-Right.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:04.334 --> 00:01:06.801
No parent would let their kid

00:01:06.801 --> 00:01:09.501
be friends with
the James Brown at 15.

00:01:09.501 --> 00:01:14.267
-I mean, you know --
Yeah, you're probably right.

00:01:14.267 --> 00:01:18.334
Yeah, him and some buddies,
him, we got together,

00:01:18.334 --> 00:01:21.501
and we learned -- what --
We learned, like,

00:01:21.501 --> 00:01:24.701
"Give Me Three Steps"
by Lynyrd Skynyrd,

00:01:24.701 --> 00:01:27.033
"Communication Breakdown,"
"Hey, Joe."

00:01:27.033 --> 00:01:29.501
And then I wrote a song.

00:01:29.501 --> 00:01:34.000
I wrote my first song at 15.
Yeah.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:35.601
-What was that
first song called?

00:01:35.601 --> 00:01:37.934
-It's called
"The Armadillo Song."

00:01:37.934 --> 00:01:39.467
-"The Armadillo Song."
-Mm-hmm.

00:01:39.467 --> 00:01:41.801
-And when you first presented it
to the rest of your bandmates,

00:01:41.801 --> 00:01:44.434
were they taken with it?

00:01:44.434 --> 00:01:46.334
-They were stoked
because it's mostly

00:01:46.334 --> 00:01:48.734
just one chord...
-Okay, gotcha.

00:01:48.734 --> 00:01:51.968
-...and it wasn't too fast,
so it wasn't hard to play.

00:01:51.968 --> 00:01:53.701
But, yeah,
it was kind of miraculous.

00:01:53.701 --> 00:01:57.067
I don't think they had
anticipated an original song.

00:01:57.067 --> 00:01:59.067
-Yeah, that's a big deal.
I mean if you're a cover band

00:01:59.067 --> 00:02:00.434
and all of a sudden,
you get a new one...

00:02:00.434 --> 00:02:03.634
What -- Do you remember it?
-I do.

00:02:03.634 --> 00:02:05.534
-Have you ever played it,
like, live?

00:02:05.534 --> 00:02:07.200
Did you guys do it at a show?
-[ Laughs ]

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:10.300
-I haven't played it
in a while, but --

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:12.000
-But you do think -- you're
confident you remember it?

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:13.133
-Mm-hmm.
I can do it.

00:02:13.133 --> 00:02:15.601
-Do you want to do it?
-Yeah, I can do it.

00:02:15.601 --> 00:02:17.200
-All right.
-I can do it.

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:20.000
-Do you, uh, have a guitar?
-Oh, well, look at this.

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:24.200
Yeah, I do.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:25.367
-That's great.

00:02:25.367 --> 00:02:26.601
I didn't even know
we had that back there.

00:02:26.601 --> 00:02:29.000
That's fantastic.
-All right. Okay.

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:31.000
-So, like, how many --
Just paint a picture.

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:33.267
Jehovah's Suspects,
what's a normal audience like?

00:02:33.267 --> 00:02:35.501
How many people
you got out there?

00:02:35.501 --> 00:02:40.834
-It's usually like
10, 15 teenagers with Dixie cups

00:02:40.834 --> 00:02:45.067
filled with some incorrect
amount of alcohol, soda pop.

00:02:45.067 --> 00:02:47.400
[ Laughter ]
-All right.

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:49.100
And is this a song
you just go into,

00:02:49.100 --> 00:02:52.567
or did you preface it all,
like, "This is a new one"...?

00:02:52.567 --> 00:02:54.067
-Ladies and gentlemen...
[ Laughter ]

00:02:54.067 --> 00:02:56.567
...I was up all night.

00:02:56.567 --> 00:02:59.934
Couldn't go to --
No, I'd just start playing.

00:02:59.934 --> 00:03:01.868
Just started playing it.

00:03:01.868 --> 00:03:06.767
♪♪

00:03:06.767 --> 00:03:12.000
♪ I wake up,
and it's a sunny day ♪

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:17.534
♪ I shake my head,
and I crawl away ♪

00:03:17.534 --> 00:03:19.868
♪ My stomach growls ♪

00:03:19.868 --> 00:03:22.567
♪ I have not been fed ♪

00:03:22.567 --> 00:03:27.334
♪ And visions of cactus pears
prick the insides of my head ♪

00:03:27.334 --> 00:03:33.167
♪ And the Earth stands still
as I punch through the heat ♪

00:03:33.167 --> 00:03:38.033
♪ The white-hot sand, it burns
the bottoms of my feet ♪

00:03:38.033 --> 00:03:44.000
♪ I'm an armadillo
with a hard shell ♪

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:46.300
♪ I look scared and ugly ♪

00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:49.000
♪ And I got a strange smell ♪

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:50.000
♪ Yeah ♪

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:51.000
[ Laughter ]

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:53.000
[ Humming ]

00:03:53.000 --> 00:04:00.901
♪♪

00:04:00.901 --> 00:04:02.734
Yeah, that was the bridge.

00:04:02.734 --> 00:04:04.734
[ Laughter ]

00:04:04.734 --> 00:04:10.767
♪♪

00:04:10.767 --> 00:04:14.300
♪ I remember the day ♪

00:04:14.300 --> 00:04:17.033
♪ When I left home ♪

00:04:17.033 --> 00:04:21.901
♪ My mama started crying,
and my pop gave me a comb ♪

00:04:21.901 --> 00:04:25.534
♪ He said,
"You got to look presentable ♪

00:04:25.534 --> 00:04:28.167
♪ Otherwise they don't care" ♪

00:04:28.167 --> 00:04:30.868
♪ He never seemed to notice ♪

00:04:30.868 --> 00:04:32.400
♪ We ain't got no hair ♪

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:34.300
[ Laughter ]

00:04:34.300 --> 00:04:36.701
♪ I miss my folks ♪

00:04:36.701 --> 00:04:38.934
♪ Don't get me wrong ♪

00:04:38.934 --> 00:04:44.300
♪ But I happen to know
that this is where I belong ♪

00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:49.801
♪ 'Cause I'm an armadillo
with a hard shell ♪

00:04:49.801 --> 00:04:51.200
♪♪

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:56.234
♪ And I was made
to roam this desolate hell ♪

00:04:56.234 --> 00:05:01.734
♪♪

00:05:01.734 --> 00:05:07.300
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:10.601
-I mean,
that's a real-ass song, Michael.

00:05:10.601 --> 00:05:11.901
[ Laughter ]

00:05:11.901 --> 00:05:13.200
-About armadillos.

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:14.300
Can I tell you something,
though?

00:05:14.300 --> 00:05:15.868
-Yeah.
-'Cause, like, I had never seen

00:05:15.868 --> 00:05:17.501
an armadillo
when I wrote that song.

00:05:17.501 --> 00:05:19.000
[ Laughter ]

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:21.968
And years later,
I was on a camping trip

00:05:21.968 --> 00:05:24.133
with one of my best friends
from Chicago.

00:05:24.133 --> 00:05:27.400
We went to Cumberland Island,
off the coast of Georgia,

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:29.267
which is covered
with armadillos,

00:05:29.267 --> 00:05:30.801
and they had tons of hair.

00:05:30.801 --> 00:05:34.167
It's just not on top of them.
It's on their underside.

00:05:34.167 --> 00:05:38.834
And then, I had to take
a long, hard look at my song,

00:05:38.834 --> 00:05:40.367
but I didn't change it.
-Yeah.

00:05:40.367 --> 00:05:42.567
-Because I didn't know
what to replace it with.

00:05:42.567 --> 00:05:45.234
-I agree. And I think, you know,
people know it that way.

00:05:45.234 --> 00:05:46.667
You're gonna have
people singing along.

00:05:46.667 --> 00:05:47.767
They're not gonna want you
to all of a sudden

00:05:47.767 --> 00:05:50.234
change out the lyrics.
-Yeah, when you're at MSG

00:05:50.234 --> 00:05:53.767
playing the song, you don't
want people getting confused.

00:05:53.767 --> 00:05:56.634
-Yeah, just for some sort of,
like, zoological accuracy.

00:05:56.634 --> 00:05:58.367
They're not there for that.
They're there to jam out.

00:05:58.367 --> 00:06:00.601
-Right, exactly.
-Did you do a little --

00:06:00.601 --> 00:06:02.901
'Cause it did seem to have
some knowledge of armadillos.

00:06:02.901 --> 00:06:04.801
Did you do a little, like,
research before your wrote --

00:06:04.801 --> 00:06:06.767
It feels like maybe, like,
you had also been assigned,

00:06:06.767 --> 00:06:08.634
like, to do
an oral report on armadillos.

00:06:08.634 --> 00:06:12.467
-I imagined, like, an armadillo
in the desert

00:06:12.467 --> 00:06:15.901
struggling to survive,

00:06:15.901 --> 00:06:19.501
which is how I very much felt
as my 15-year-old self

00:06:19.501 --> 00:06:20.834
could identify with that.

00:06:20.834 --> 00:06:22.467
-See, this is how
you should introduce the song.

00:06:22.467 --> 00:06:25.434
-Yeah, yeah. Well, I don't want
to make people cry, but...

00:06:25.434 --> 00:06:26.901
[ Laughter ]

00:06:26.901 --> 00:06:28.868
But, uh...

00:06:28.868 --> 00:06:31.501
But then, like I said,
I went to this island.

00:06:31.501 --> 00:06:34.200
It was a very lush island,
not a desert,

00:06:34.200 --> 00:06:37.300
and all the armadillos
were covered with hair.

00:06:37.300 --> 00:06:39.234
-[ Laughs ]
-And it just goes to show

00:06:39.234 --> 00:06:43.334
that how little grasp of reality
any of us really have.

00:06:43.334 --> 00:06:46.100
[ Laughter ]

00:06:46.100 --> 00:06:48.334
-That is --
I mean, yeah, they don't

00:06:48.334 --> 00:06:49.767
tell you that at
Ripley's Believe It or Not.

00:06:49.767 --> 00:06:52.801
-No.
"Can you believe it?

00:06:52.801 --> 00:06:55.367
Armadillos have hair!"
[ Laughter ]

00:06:55.367 --> 00:06:58.234
-Hey, thank you so much
for giving us the world premiere

00:06:58.234 --> 00:06:59.801
of "The Armadillo Song."
-Changing your life.

00:06:59.801 --> 00:07:01.801
-Changing my life.
I was gonna say that, too.

00:07:01.801 --> 00:07:03.300
New episodes of
"Nine Perfect Strangers"

00:07:03.300 --> 00:07:04.801
premiere Wednesdays on Hulu.

